Here's the updated README based on everything I now know about your final system:

Mill River Visual Analytics
Paradise Pond · Smith College · Northampton, MA
2019 – 2024

Purpose
This visual analytics system supports long-term biological monitoring of the Mill River upstream and downstream of Paradise Pond at Smith College. The data tracks macroinvertebrate communities — small aquatic organisms that serve as biological indicators of river health — collected each summer and fall from 2019 to 2024.
The system is designed to help researchers understand:

How macroinvertebrate communities and functional feeding groups change across seasons and years
How environmental variables (conductivity, water temperature, stream discharge, etc.) relate to organism density
Which organisms show unusual or anomalous responses to environmental conditions

Researcher: Marney Pratt, Biology Department, Smith College
Dataset: 238 species · 84 families · Upstream & Downstream locations · Summer & Fall seasons · 2019–2023

How to Use
Running the System
Because the system loads local JSON data files, it must be served over a local HTTP server — opening the HTML file directly in a browser will not work.

Make sure Python is installed
Open a terminal and navigate to the project folder:

bashcd /path/to/project

Start a local server:

bashpython3 -m http.server 8000

Open your browser and go to http://localhost:8000

Navigating the System
Use the three buttons at the top to switch between views.

1. Communities Across Seasons
Shows 10 side-by-side scatterplots organized by year (2019–2023) and season (Summer left, Fall right). Each plot shows Water Temperature (x-axis) vs Log scale of Density of macroinvertebrates (y-axis). Points are colored by functional feeding group.

Use the brush tool on the Summer 2019 plot — click and drag to draw a rectangle around dots
Matching dots will highlight with a red outline across all other plots
This lets you track whether the same organisms appear at similar densities and temperatures across different seasons and years
Each row is labeled with the year on the left side
A legend at the top shows the feeding group color mapping


2. Environmental Effects

Use the dropdowns on the left to select an environmental variable and a functional feeding group
Two scatterplots appear side by side — Upstream (left) and Downstream (right)
Each point represents an organism observation; density is shown on a log scale
Points are colored by functional feeding group
The shared axis scale between the two plots allows direct comparison of upstream vs downstream patterns


3. Anomalous Organisms

Shows a correlation matrix of organism families vs. environmental variables
Colored cells indicate a notable correlation (blue = positive, red = negative)
Check "Show anomalies only" to filter to rows with |r| > 0.3
Click any colored cell to see a detailed scatter plot on the right showing that organism's density vs the selected environmental variable


Known Bugs

The brush interaction in Communities Across Seasons only listens for selections made on the Summer 2019 (top left) plot — brushing on other plots will not trigger highlights
On first load, the Environmental Effects view shows blank plots until both dropdowns are selected
The detail scatter plot in Anomalous Organisms uses a linear scale; extreme density outliers can compress most points toward the bottom


Data Cleaning & Analysis
Source Data
Raw data was collected by Marney Pratt's lab from field samples taken upstream and downstream of Paradise Pond each summer and fall, 2019–2023.
Cleaning Steps

Records with missing or non-finite values for environmental variables or density were excluded from visualizations
Only observations with density > 0 were included in the scatterplots
Family and feeding group labels were standardized to consistent casing for matching across files

Derived Data

merged_data.json — cleaned and merged observation records including organism, family, feeding group, location, season, year, density, and environmental measurements
correlations.json — Pearson correlation coefficients computed between each organism family's density and each environmental variable, calculated across all available observations


Dependencies

D3.js v7 (included locally as d3.v7.min.js)
No other external libraries required


